WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.550 --> 00:00:05.620
Kenneth Castillo, Filmmaker/Author: One thing
in my book I said one thing I kept reading

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:10.960
from “industry insiders” and it bothers
me every time is there are three things to

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.270
success and that is be kind, be humble, and
help other people succeed.

00:00:16.270 --> 00:00:19.800
If you’re do those three things you’re
going to succeed in our business and I just

00:00:19.800 --> 00:00:21.140
think that is such bull.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.380
Because I’m like if those are the three
things, that’s what you would use to get

00:00:24.380 --> 00:00:27.829
into the business, then your journey wasn’t
very difficult.

00:00:27.829 --> 00:00:31.579
My experience has taught me that when I’ve
been kind, I get taken advantage of.

00:00:31.579 --> 00:00:32.579
Film Courage: True.

00:00:32.579 --> 00:00:35.340
Kenneth: When I’m humble, people don’t
take me seriously as a filmmaker and when

00:00:35.340 --> 00:00:39.809
I try and help other people they end up just
using me as a stepping stone.

00:00:39.809 --> 00:00:40.809
Film Courage: Very true.

00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:44.890
Kenneth: I’m not saying those aren’t three
things you should be in life but in this business

00:00:44.890 --> 00:00:50.590
if you’re starting from nothing you are
going to have a very hard time in this business.

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:55.929
But these are all people who kind of had a
foot in the door you know what I mean (that

00:00:55.929 --> 00:00:56.929
say these things)?

00:00:56.929 --> 00:00:57.929
Film Courage: Yes.

00:00:57.929 --> 00:01:00.539
Kenneth: And when you don’t have a foot
in the door well those things will be valuable

00:01:00.539 --> 00:01:06.400
to you maybe everyone is used to dealing with
a jerk but you are already somebody and there’s

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:11.040
an agent’s blog that I follow but I called
him out on that and he didn’t respond.

00:01:11.040 --> 00:01:15.040
But I was just kind of like…I go your last
name is a famous last name, you are the son

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:22.000
of a famous agent so you already have a door
open to you so being those three things maybe

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:25.040
that disarms people and they go oh yeah, okay.

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:29.270
But when you’re successful already, absolutely
that works because everyone goes Oh wow?

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:33.260
They are so kind, they are so humble and they’re
helping other people.

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.860
But you are already successful, it’s easy.

00:01:35.860 --> 00:01:39.910
You’re not going to loose anything by being
that and especially if most people are used

00:01:39.910 --> 00:01:44.040
to jerks in those positions but yeah I’m
going on.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.080
Film Courage: That’s fine but I do agree
with you.

00:01:46.080 --> 00:01:47.550
I think in life those are good rules.

00:01:47.550 --> 00:01:48.550
Kenneth: Great rules.

00:01:48.550 --> 00:01:53.030
Film Courage: I think in this business, if
you’re going to help somebody you do it

00:01:53.030 --> 00:01:57.910
because you want to do it but there is not…I
don’t know?

00:01:57.910 --> 00:02:00.880
Kenneth: That could be a whole other workshop.

00:02:00.880 --> 00:02:06.000
Film Courage: And I think people who would
disagree and say that’s being too negative,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.950
I think a lot of them probably haven’t been
through a lot.

00:02:09.950 --> 00:02:12.490
Kenneth: They are naive in a negative way
in terms of the business.

00:02:12.490 --> 00:02:17.120
Film Courage: Right, if you want to do the
right thing and you want to keep your word

00:02:17.120 --> 00:02:22.450
(I think that’s great), what about in search
of fairness in this business and thinking

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.000
that everyone is going to play that way?

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:30.210
Kenneth: Well I would say this, whatever it
is that you’re doing, if you’re a writer,

00:02:30.210 --> 00:02:35.569
an actor, a director there is the discipline
and craft of doing those things and learning

00:02:35.569 --> 00:02:41.390
those things (constantly learning) but it’s
also looking and learning and paying attention

00:02:41.390 --> 00:02:46.790
to this as a business not in the sense that
it will get you jaded but to give you some

00:02:46.790 --> 00:02:48.980
type…don’t go in naive.

00:02:48.980 --> 00:02:50.060
It is a business.

00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:51.450
That’s number one.

00:02:51.450 --> 00:02:55.880
I’ve gotten into major discussions with
distributors over my posters because they

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.380
weren’t artistic enough and I wanted an
artistic poster and they just wanted to put

00:02:59.380 --> 00:03:04.330
Danny Trejo on the cover because that’s
what is going to sell and that’s what they

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.330
do right?

00:03:05.330 --> 00:03:09.900
So I have to let them do what they do, they
let me do what I do which is make the movie

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:13.150
but there is no fairness in this business,
there isn’t.

00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:19.580
And if you understand that going in then I
think you can deal with it a lot better but

00:03:19.580 --> 00:03:23.560
if you go in thinking something is going to
make sense or your journey is going to make

00:03:23.560 --> 00:03:28.030
sense or it’s going to be fair or the outcome
is going to be fair you’re going to have

00:03:28.030 --> 00:03:32.440
a very difficult time because there is nothing
fair.

00:03:32.440 --> 00:03:37.690
I know actors that here and have been at it
for 40 years and have never been a series

00:03:37.690 --> 00:03:38.690
regular.

00:03:38.690 --> 00:03:42.319
I have actors who’ve come out here and have
been out here 10 years and are series regulars.

00:03:42.319 --> 00:03:43.570
Is that fair?

00:03:43.570 --> 00:03:45.940
No, but that’s just the nature of the business.

00:03:45.940 --> 00:03:50.400
It attracts a lot of different people from
all over the world that all have something

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:56.330
to say and that can actually be an empowering
thing because to me there is no one way to

00:03:56.330 --> 00:03:57.330
get in.

00:03:57.330 --> 00:04:02.700
If you took a panel of five filmmakers they
would each…let’s say five very successful

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:05.200
filmmakers, each of them would have a different
way of how they got in.

00:04:05.200 --> 00:04:10.110
I don’t think there are two stories that
are the same of how people succeed in this

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.220
business.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:16.820
You just have to find what your story is and
commit to your journey and try not to make

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.289
it like anybody else.

00:04:18.289 --> 00:04:24.639
But go in there with the understanding how
the business works to the best of your ability

00:04:24.639 --> 00:04:30.990
and unfortunately a lot of that experience
comes through failure and so you have to look

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.120
at it that way.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.090
I always say the more no’s I get, it gets
me closer to a yes.

00:04:36.090 --> 00:04:39.139
That’s a pretty true statement.

00:04:39.139 --> 00:04:42.460
There are some emails I sent out that were
that were not worded right when I reached

00:04:42.460 --> 00:04:46.509
out to the show runner and it got back to
me and I’m like you know what, I probably

00:04:46.509 --> 00:04:48.949
could have worded that better.

00:04:48.949 --> 00:04:50.090
But that’s a learning thing.

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:51.110
I didn’t look at it as a negative.

00:04:51.110 --> 00:04:54.860
I looked at it as well maybe I should have
asked for that or maybe I shouldn’t have

00:04:54.860 --> 00:05:00.800
worded it that way or maybe I should have
reached out directly to that person but I

00:05:00.800 --> 00:05:05.449
don’t know that there is any short of just
being a complete douche bag in this business

00:05:05.449 --> 00:05:08.009
I don’t know if that any little mistakes
that you’re going to make that are going

00:05:08.009 --> 00:05:09.589
to be so detrimental to your career.

00:05:09.589 --> 00:05:11.029
I’ve made a ton.

00:05:11.029 --> 00:05:16.559
It hasn’t kept me from producing, writing
and directing because it’s so democratized

00:05:16.559 --> 00:05:17.559
now.

00:05:17.559 --> 00:05:21.860
Now you can shoot one on your phone and you
can learn how to shoot one on your phone,

00:05:21.860 --> 00:05:22.939
you know what I mean?

00:05:22.939 --> 00:05:23.939
So it’s like you don’t know how?

00:05:23.939 --> 00:05:24.939
You can look it up.

00:05:24.939 --> 00:05:25.939
How do I make a movie?

00:05:25.939 --> 00:05:28.340
How do I make a short film?

00:05:28.340 --> 00:05:36.139
So you have to be constantly reading and researching
and you got to love it, even the warts.

00:05:36.139 --> 00:05:38.139
Because if you don’t it will just eat you.

00:05:38.139 --> 00:05:48.469
I’ve seen it happen to people and it’s
not to good people because they were so eaten

00:05:48.469 --> 00:05:51.539
alive by the fact that it’s not fair, this
isn’t fair.

00:05:51.539 --> 00:05:55.189
I did all of this work and I sacrificed so
much.

00:05:55.189 --> 00:05:56.639
This isn’t fair!

00:05:56.639 --> 00:06:00.610
Why did their movie get here and my movie
didn’t?

00:06:00.610 --> 00:06:06.050
Film Courage: So if someone is in that loop,
it’s late at night, it’s early morning

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:10.860
- they are waking up and are like “This
isn’t fair but if I stop I don’t get to

00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:12.300
do X, Y or Z.

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:18.229
I may not get a [festival] laurel and I may
not get this publication to pick me as their

00:06:18.229 --> 00:06:20.889
new top 25 for the website but…”

00:06:20.889 --> 00:06:23.469
Kenneth: That’s funny because I’m not
that guy.

00:06:23.469 --> 00:06:27.189
I’ve never been that guy who got on a top
25 list.

00:06:27.189 --> 00:06:33.289
I’m not the guy who is a film festival guy,
but I am the guy who has written and directed

00:06:33.289 --> 00:06:34.440
several feature films.

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.800
That’s the guy I want to be.

00:06:36.800 --> 00:06:42.229
So those are goals and interesting goals but
if you have a short film and you’re going

00:06:42.229 --> 00:06:48.220
all over the world like a friend of mine is
a filmmaker who went to film school, had an

00:06:48.220 --> 00:06:50.830
award-winning short, decided he wanted to
go to AFI because he thought he would make

00:06:50.830 --> 00:06:52.039
more connections that way.

00:06:52.039 --> 00:06:53.620
I go “You already know how to make a movie.

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:58.599
Why are you going to go spend $100,000 on
AFI?”

00:06:58.599 --> 00:06:59.889
And I’m like “That’s no guarantee man.

00:06:59.889 --> 00:07:03.899
Yeah, you will meet some people but don’t
you think everybody is going for that reason?”

00:07:03.899 --> 00:07:06.849
And I just go make something.

00:07:06.849 --> 00:07:08.860
How much money did you spend going all over
the world with your films?

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.039
You’ve already won a ton of awards oh yeah
but you didn’t get the next project.

00:07:12.039 --> 00:07:14.250
I go I would have taken all of that money
and made something with it.

00:07:14.250 --> 00:07:18.539
It depends on what your priority is.

00:07:18.539 --> 00:07:22.659
I think if you’re ego driven, maybe that’s
what drive you?

00:07:22.659 --> 00:07:23.659
I don’t know?

00:07:23.659 --> 00:07:24.659
I’m just not that person.

00:07:24.659 --> 00:07:26.419
I’m driven by storytelling pure and simple.

00:07:26.419 --> 00:07:30.619
I’m not a multi-hyphenate either, I say
writer/director so people have some type of

00:07:30.619 --> 00:07:36.279
perspective for what I do but essentially
I just see myself as a storyteller and I’m

00:07:36.279 --> 00:07:41.610
using the tools that I have at my disposal
to tell the stories that I want to tell and

00:07:41.610 --> 00:07:43.050
that’s what drives me.

00:07:43.050 --> 00:07:44.729
None of the other stuff does.

00:07:44.729 --> 00:07:46.479
All the other stuff would be great.

00:07:46.479 --> 00:07:50.439
You know my very simple goal is to just make
a living as a writer/director.

00:07:50.439 --> 00:07:52.999
That doesn’t just necessarily mean going
to Sundance.

00:07:52.999 --> 00:07:57.339
That doesn’t necessarily mean being rich
and successful.

00:07:57.339 --> 00:07:59.569
It just means making a living as a writer/director.

00:07:59.569 --> 00:08:01.809
That’s an attainable goal.

00:08:01.809 --> 00:08:07.039
Everything else if that happens that’s all
gravy but an attainable goal for me is that

00:08:07.039 --> 00:08:13.839
and your first time out you are expecting
look around, pay attention.

00:08:13.839 --> 00:08:17.913
I’m looking at the hardest thing with Marigold
the Matador [Kenneth’s feature film from

00:08:17.913 --> 00:08:20.790
2018] was I was making an undistributable
and I knew it.

00:08:20.790 --> 00:08:26.339
It wasn’t my first film, it was my seventh
and I knew it was undistributable and having

00:08:26.339 --> 00:08:32.589
to keep going because the itch would not go
away on telling this story was very difficult.

00:08:32.589 --> 00:08:36.870
Whereas if I had a naivety of a first time
filmmaker I would have plowed right through

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:37.870
that right?

00:08:37.870 --> 00:08:42.849
Because I am making my first film but I’m
making my seventh and I am broke and I am

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:44.180
going “Why am I doing this?”

00:08:44.180 --> 00:08:45.610
It’s just something that I have to do.

00:08:45.610 --> 00:08:48.800
Film Courage: Why was it undistributable?

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:50.959
Kenneth: For many reasons.

00:08:50.959 --> 00:08:55.500
Just going from off of my experience it’s
about a single woman, there’s a homeless

00:08:55.500 --> 00:08:59.920
character and he’s schizophrenic and it’s
like a coming-of-age story of this young girl,

00:08:59.920 --> 00:09:01.560
it’s an all latin cast.

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:05.560
It takes place in Los Angeles in what I call
“The Real LA.”

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:09.220
There are no stars in it.

00:09:09.220 --> 00:09:10.260
Camila Banus is in it.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:11.380
She’s an amazing actor.

00:09:11.380 --> 00:09:13.720
She’s on Days of Our Lives and she’s do
good.

00:09:13.720 --> 00:09:15.779
She was so gracious to be a part of that project.

00:09:15.779 --> 00:09:20.980
That was my second time working with her.

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:24.740
Knowing the experience I had and what distributors
say I just didn’t feel like it was going

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:28.420
to find a buyer, at least not right now?

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:33.779
Maybe later down the line they will be, we
will see.

00:09:33.779 --> 00:09:37.561
When Alfonso Cuarón’s movie Roma came out
I thought it’s very much shot in that vein,

00:09:37.561 --> 00:09:44.959
not to that brilliance obviously but it was
a great movie about a story about a woman

00:09:44.959 --> 00:09:51.050
who wasn’t a star and everything else and
it did pretty well I think so who knows?

00:09:51.050 --> 00:09:56.751
But again I don’t have the machine behind
me so I just have to sit and wait on that.

00:09:56.751 --> 00:09:57.751
Question for the Viewers:

00:09:57.751 --> 00:09:57.760
What are your key takeaways from this video
segment?

